# The complexity job's tools (.github/workflows/complexity.yml): Lizard and Pylint, the two Codacy
# runs whose findings its pull-request check misses, and Prospector, which Codacy runs on every
# Python file EXCEPT those over 150 KB (see .github/scripts/complexity_gate.py). Wheels only,
# hash-checked.
#
# pip-compile turns this into complexity.txt: every package, exact versions AND hashes, installed
# with `--require-hashes`. Dependabot keeps it current by re-running pip-compile on the Python its
# header names (3.11). Edit THIS file, then regenerate (run in python:3.11-slim):
#   pip-compile --generate-hashes --no-annotate --strip-extras --unsafe-package=pylint-celery \
#     --output-file=complexity.txt complexity.in
#
# pylint-celery is left out (--unsafe-package) because it ships no wheel, only an sdist, and every
# install here is wheels only. Prospector declares it unconditionally but loads it only as a Pylint
# plugin for a project that uses Celery, and .prospector.yaml runs no Pylint, so the job installs
# complexity.txt with --no-deps: the file is the whole closure apart from that one package. The
# gate's self-test runs Prospector before its verdict counts, so a bump that needs a package this
# file lacks fails the job rather than passing it. Dependabot keeps --unsafe-package (it reads it
# back from the header). Without --allow-unsafe on purpose: with it, pip-compile pins the unsafe
# package anyway.
lizard
pylint
# Prospector at the version Codacy runs (codacy/codacy-prospector requirements.txt:
# prospector[with_everything]==1.19.1), so the PR side counts what Codacy would have counted.
prospector==1.19.1
